WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.466 --> 00:00:06.566
Narrator: From oil paintings
blackened by tobacco smoke

00:00:06.566 --> 00:00:09.733
to broken century-old marble statues,

00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:12.000
from books ripping apart at the seams

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.900
to faded Italian murals,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.166
these items were in
desperate need of repairs.

00:00:17.166 --> 00:00:19.566
We spoke with specialists to find out

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:24.133
how they repair and restore
centuries-old works of art.

00:00:36.766 --> 00:00:40.133
My name is Sarah Drew, and
I work as an art conservator

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:43.033
at Center Art Studio in New York City.

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.266
This is a portrait of Madame Vestris.

00:00:53.566 --> 00:00:56.366
The first thing we do
is inspect the painting

00:00:56.366 --> 00:01:01.166
with a blacklight to understand
what all the issues are.

00:01:01.166 --> 00:01:02.900
So you see the purple light,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.966
and then you see the kinda,
like, the greenish light?

00:01:04.966 --> 00:01:07.500
That indicates to us that
there's, like a heavy,

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.866
thick old varnish on that surface.

00:01:09.866 --> 00:01:12.833
If there wasn't that, you
would just see pure purple.

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:17.100
You couldn't make out any sort of contrast

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.766
or light in the background.

00:01:18.766 --> 00:01:21.633
The ornament and the
details on her clothes

00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:23.433
were completely obscured.

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:24.833
There was something on her head;

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.033
we weren't sure if it was
a hat or if it was hair.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:29.800
When you're running the
Q-tip over the painting,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.100
it can be quite black or quite
orange or yellow depending

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.133
on what you're taking off.

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:46.166
Her skin was beautiful.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.933
It was bright, it had brushstrokes,
color contrast, light.

00:02:01.266 --> 00:02:04.666
The first step of removing the
painting from the stretcher

00:02:04.666 --> 00:02:07.400
is to take out all of the tacks

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.333
that are holding the sides of
the canvas onto the stretcher.

00:02:11.333 --> 00:02:14.300
I'll save them and I'm
gonna reuse them later.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.100
You can see me using a knife
to separate the lining canvas

00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:28.400
fully from the structure.

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:50.200
Now comes the riskiest part,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.766
separating the painting
from the stretcher.

00:02:52.766 --> 00:02:54.766
You want to be very careful

00:02:54.766 --> 00:02:57.333
because if the paint is not stable,

00:02:58.233 --> 00:03:03.233
you risk bending the
painting and causing flaking

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:05.933
or further damage.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.200
I need to cut strips of linen

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:33.133
and attach them to the canvas

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:37.033
so that I can then put the
whole piece back together again.

00:03:37.033 --> 00:03:42.033
I cut strips of BEVA, which
is a conservator's adhesive.

00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:04.500
I then put the painting on the stretcher

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.500
and use tacks to reattach
the painting to its stretcher

00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:24.400
Then I filled, with a water
based putty, areas of damage.

00:04:33.966 --> 00:04:37.100
And I overfill the areas a little bit,

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.733
and then I come back in
with a damp cotton swab

00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:42.766
and clean up those fills.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.500
Also, the original canvas
doesn't extend quite to the edges

00:04:50.666 --> 00:04:53.133
of the stretcher so I need to go and fill

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:54.866
about a quarter of an inch

00:04:54.866 --> 00:04:58.533
around the entire
perimeter of the painting.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.866
Once the painting is filled,

00:05:06.866 --> 00:05:09.833
I will begin the retouching process.

00:05:16.366 --> 00:05:20.100
I will use a paint that
is specifically designed

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.066
for conservation work.

00:05:38.833 --> 00:05:41.466
You're just trying to match the color

00:05:41.466 --> 00:05:44.033
to the color that the artist put there,

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.400
and making sure that
you're not painting over

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.366
any of that original paint.

00:05:48.366 --> 00:05:51.600
There's two types of
inpainting that I will do here.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.333
The first is inpainting over
the fills that I just made.

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:59.000
The second is inpainting the background.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.233
And this is the area that
was previously overcleaned

00:06:02.233 --> 00:06:03.800
by the other restorer.

00:06:05.566 --> 00:06:09.366
It's really important to
use a conservation paint

00:06:09.366 --> 00:06:13.133
that dissolves in a
solvent that is different

00:06:13.133 --> 00:06:15.566
than the original paint.

00:06:15.566 --> 00:06:20.033
And this allows our paint
to be completely reversible.

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:23.466
We could easily use that
solvent to take off our paint

00:06:23.466 --> 00:06:27.233
and then the original
paint would be unaffected.

00:06:27.233 --> 00:06:30.233
Then I will be ready for varnish.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:53.200
We use a completely reversible
non-yellowing varnish.

00:06:54.166 --> 00:06:57.233
I need to wipe off the
surface of the painting

00:06:57.233 --> 00:07:00.000
with just a cloth because, like anything,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.233
it can accumulate little
particles of dust, little hairs.

00:07:03.233 --> 00:07:06.733
I wanna make sure that that
surface is as clean as possible.

00:07:07.733 --> 00:07:11.466
So once I wipe it off, I
will brush on the varnish.

00:07:13.166 --> 00:07:14.933
When you brush on the varnish,

00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:19.933
those fills, that inpainting,
it all kind of blends

00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:22.300
cohesively into the background

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.833
and into the surface of the painting

00:07:24.833 --> 00:07:27.066
and it just looks complete.

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:50.400
And the restoration is complete.

00:08:48.766 --> 00:08:51.166
[soft music]

00:08:55.066 --> 00:08:58.666
I'm restoring a portrait of Edmund Holland

00:08:58.666 --> 00:09:01.566
that was painted in 1895.

00:09:01.566 --> 00:09:04.066
This painting is an oil on canvas,

00:09:04.066 --> 00:09:07.866
and it is in a painted
and gilt wood frame.

00:09:07.866 --> 00:09:10.666
Both the painting and the
frame are extremely dirty.

00:09:10.666 --> 00:09:14.766
The painting has a very
yellow varnish on its surface,

00:09:14.766 --> 00:09:17.766
and the frame has many cracks and chips

00:09:17.766 --> 00:09:20.833
and loose areas that we will fix.

00:09:20.833 --> 00:09:24.666
So, the first thing is to unbox it

00:09:24.666 --> 00:09:27.800
when it arrives and to
do an initial assessment,

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.500
take note of any condition issues,

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:34.466
and then you start the
deconstruction process.

00:09:34.466 --> 00:09:35.766
We're going to work on the painting

00:09:35.766 --> 00:09:37.166
and the frame separately.

00:09:37.166 --> 00:09:40.766
The painting is held into
the frame with nails,

00:09:40.766 --> 00:09:44.400
so I used pliers to bend the nails out,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.533
and it's pretty easy.

00:09:46.533 --> 00:09:47.866
There were only a few of them.

00:09:47.866 --> 00:09:50.566
I'm going to work on
restoring the painting.

00:09:50.566 --> 00:09:53.866
My colleague Caryn is going to
work on restoring the frame.

00:09:53.866 --> 00:09:58.200
So, we bring the frame into our wood room

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.966
so Caryn can get started working on that.

00:10:00.966 --> 00:10:05.566
I begin by taking the
painting off the stretcher.

00:10:05.566 --> 00:10:06.966
So, in order to do this,

00:10:06.966 --> 00:10:10.733
I remove the tacks along
the sides of the stretcher

00:10:10.733 --> 00:10:14.633
that are holding the canvas
tightly onto the stretcher.

00:10:14.633 --> 00:10:18.000
I use pliers to pull these tacks out,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.633
and that loosens the painting

00:10:20.633 --> 00:10:24.266
and allows it to be a free
piece of canvas again.

00:10:24.266 --> 00:10:26.966
When the painting comes off the stretcher,

00:10:26.966 --> 00:10:31.333
it's not uncommon for a
painting to not lie flat

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:35.266
because it's been held bent for decades.

00:10:35.266 --> 00:10:39.466
So I cut a piece of silicone release paper

00:10:39.466 --> 00:10:41.866
and use a small tacking iron

00:10:41.866 --> 00:10:46.833
to iron down the edges of
the canvas so it lays flat.

00:10:47.766 --> 00:10:50.433
I do an initial vacuum to remove

00:10:50.433 --> 00:10:53.633
all the bulky, loose, obvious dust

00:10:53.633 --> 00:10:56.066
that is accumulated on
the back of the canvas

00:10:56.066 --> 00:10:57.566
and the front of the canvas.

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:02.666
After I vacuum, I use a
vulcanized rubber sponge

00:11:02.666 --> 00:11:06.166
to remove more surface dust

00:11:06.166 --> 00:11:09.166
and dirt that is a
little bit more ingrained

00:11:09.166 --> 00:11:11.966
into the surface of the canvas.

00:11:11.966 --> 00:11:15.066
This is a specially made sponge

00:11:15.066 --> 00:11:16.933
that is very porous

00:11:16.933 --> 00:11:20.866
and pulls dirt off of surfaces

00:11:20.866 --> 00:11:24.166
that might be slightly more
ingrained into the surfaces.

00:11:24.166 --> 00:11:26.500
We use light pressure with the sponge,

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.566
and we tend to go

00:11:28.566 --> 00:11:32.200
in straight lines

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.866
as opposed to circular
or scrubbing patterns.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:39.700
We do a surfactant cleaning

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:44.233
to remove grease and
grime and oily substances

00:11:44.233 --> 00:11:46.133
off the surface of the painting.

00:11:46.133 --> 00:11:47.766
At this point, I'm
still not doing anything

00:11:47.766 --> 00:11:50.900
to affect the varnish
or the underlying paint

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:51.966
or anything like that.

00:11:51.966 --> 00:11:55.700
We use cotton swabs dipped in a solution

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:58.433
of the surfactant, soap,
and water, typically.

00:11:58.433 --> 00:12:01.900
Wet the swab, and then wipe the swab

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.700
in typically circular motions

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.400
over small areas of the canvas at a time.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.033
And this is a very controlled way

00:12:10.033 --> 00:12:14.433
to remove the sticky, grimy substances

00:12:14.433 --> 00:12:17.533
that accumulate on a
painting surface over time.

00:12:17.533 --> 00:12:21.066
The soap solution also removed overpaint

00:12:21.066 --> 00:12:22.800
from a previous restorer.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.266
There are a number of tears in the canvas

00:12:26.266 --> 00:12:29.466
that have been repaired by somebody else,

00:12:29.466 --> 00:12:31.466
and they were not repaired very well.

00:12:31.466 --> 00:12:34.900
So I removed all that off.

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:37.266
[scraping]

00:12:37.266 --> 00:12:39.266
Underneath, the previous restorer

00:12:39.266 --> 00:12:40.900
had put a layer of paper

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:42.600
on the surface of the painting,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:44.633
which is an odd thing to do.

00:12:44.633 --> 00:12:47.133
So I used a scalpel to scrape that off,

00:12:47.133 --> 00:12:49.266
but between the heat gun and the scalpel,

00:12:49.266 --> 00:12:52.200
we can scrape off the paper,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.866
and we will repair these areas

00:12:54.866 --> 00:12:56.900
by patching them on the back

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:01.100
and using a putty to
fill in just the crack

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:03.833
and make sure that we are not covering

00:13:03.833 --> 00:13:06.066
over any of the original paint.

00:13:06.066 --> 00:13:08.166
Once I've done my surfactant cleaning,

00:13:08.166 --> 00:13:11.933
I am ready to assess the
painting with a black light

00:13:11.933 --> 00:13:14.733
and do some solvent tests

00:13:14.733 --> 00:13:17.733
to see what will cut through the varnish

00:13:17.733 --> 00:13:20.133
and get down to the original paint layer.

00:13:20.133 --> 00:13:24.400
I use an ultraviolet black
light to assess the surface.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:27.866
And this light allows me to see things

00:13:27.866 --> 00:13:29.966
that are going on under
the surface of the varnish

00:13:29.966 --> 00:13:32.666
that might not be apparent to a naked eye.

00:13:32.666 --> 00:13:35.133
I assemble various solvents

00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:38.500
that I believe will be
contenders for cleaning.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:41.766
And I do very small test cleanings

00:13:41.766 --> 00:13:44.633
in discrete areas on the canvas

00:13:44.633 --> 00:13:48.900
to test and work out a plan

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:52.000
for cleaning the entire
surface of the canvas.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.966
And I check my work with a black light

00:13:54.966 --> 00:13:57.800
to make sure that what
I'm doing is working.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:01.733
If I shine the black light
over my test cleaning,

00:14:01.733 --> 00:14:05.400
I want to see that that
test-cleaning window

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.166
is reflecting back purple.

00:14:07.166 --> 00:14:09.966
That means that I've cut
through that varnish,

00:14:09.966 --> 00:14:11.633
I've gotten down to the original paint,

00:14:11.633 --> 00:14:15.300
and it informs me and
makes me feel comfortable

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.866
moving forward to work in larger areas.

00:14:17.866 --> 00:14:20.033
But similar to the surfactant cleaning,

00:14:20.033 --> 00:14:23.200
I use large cotton swabs to work my way

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.700
over the entire surface of the painting,

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:27.600
and so satisfying to see

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:30.466
all the old yellow varnish come off.

00:14:30.466 --> 00:14:33.166
I saved the face for
last in this instance,

00:14:33.166 --> 00:14:35.266
mostly because it's the most dramatic part

00:14:35.266 --> 00:14:36.366
of the cleaning process,

00:14:36.366 --> 00:14:38.633
and so it's good motivation to keep going.

00:14:38.633 --> 00:14:39.766
And we want to get to the face,

00:14:39.766 --> 00:14:41.966
so that's the most fun part to clean.

00:14:41.966 --> 00:14:45.766
It also became apparent once
I removed all the varnish

00:14:45.766 --> 00:14:48.033
that this painting,
which had been treated by

00:14:48.033 --> 00:14:51.566
this other restorer, had
been cleaned previously.

00:14:51.566 --> 00:14:54.133
However, it was cleaned a
little too aggressively.

00:14:54.133 --> 00:14:56.066
So there were areas where

00:14:56.066 --> 00:14:58.566
the original paint had become thin.

00:14:58.566 --> 00:15:01.633
So, now I'm realizing that I have

00:15:01.633 --> 00:15:04.000
maybe a lot more retouching in my future

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.300
than I maybe anticipated.

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:08.533
Once the painting is clean,

00:15:08.533 --> 00:15:11.466
we remove the old patches

00:15:11.466 --> 00:15:14.200
and the old strip-lining canvas

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:16.033
that's on the back of the painting.

00:15:16.033 --> 00:15:19.500
Lansing, who is the owner of the studio,

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:21.366
is doing this part.

00:15:21.366 --> 00:15:25.566
Lansing is taking off the old
patches and the old strips.

00:15:25.566 --> 00:15:28.300
To do this, he uses a hot-air gun

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:30.600
to heat up the patches,

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.000
and this loosens up the older pieces

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.566
and allows Lansing to take a metal tool

00:15:35.566 --> 00:15:37.433
to lift off the patch.

00:15:37.433 --> 00:15:40.500
And we're left with the original canvas.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.566
We will strip line the
edges of the canvas.

00:15:43.566 --> 00:15:45.333
This is so we will be able to

00:15:45.333 --> 00:15:48.266
restretch the painting
tightly onto the stretcher.

00:15:49.466 --> 00:15:51.966
We cut strips of linen

00:15:51.966 --> 00:15:56.833
and adhere conservator's
adhesive to the strips,

00:15:56.833 --> 00:15:59.166
and this is heat activated.

00:15:59.166 --> 00:16:02.100
So we use an iron to attach these strips

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.566
to the back of the original canvas.

00:16:04.566 --> 00:16:07.000
We also will do the same technique

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.500
for the patches that are on the back,

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.700
and these patches will repair

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:15.833
the rips that are in the canvas' surface.

00:16:15.833 --> 00:16:20.000
We glue down the frayed
edges of the strip lining

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:24.300
so they also lay flat
against the original canvas.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:27.666
Lansing will restretch the painting

00:16:27.666 --> 00:16:30.400
onto its original structure.

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:34.100
He marks the middle of all
four sides of the painting

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:36.100
and all four sides of the stretcher,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.633
and then he lays the loose
canvas onto the stretcher,

00:16:39.633 --> 00:16:43.266
aligns the marks, and then
begins the stretching process.

00:16:43.266 --> 00:16:47.666
He will use a tacking hammer
and the original tacks

00:16:47.666 --> 00:16:50.266
that I removed earlier that we saved

00:16:50.266 --> 00:16:53.566
to reattach the canvas to the stretcher.

00:16:55.166 --> 00:16:57.766
Now my colleague Tyler will be applying

00:16:57.766 --> 00:17:00.133
a layer of isolation varnish

00:17:00.133 --> 00:17:01.533
to the painting surface

00:17:01.533 --> 00:17:03.966
to protect all the original paint

00:17:03.966 --> 00:17:06.866
and create a barrier
between the original paint

00:17:06.866 --> 00:17:10.233
and the paint and materials
that I'm going to apply.

00:17:10.233 --> 00:17:14.400
We're using a reversible,
nonyellowing conservator's varnish

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:15.800
that's easily removable.

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:19.300
We brush it onto the
painting surface all over

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.400
and allow it to dry.

00:17:23.366 --> 00:17:27.433
At this point, we put the keys
in the back of the stretcher

00:17:27.433 --> 00:17:31.466
and tap the keys to make
the surface of the painting

00:17:31.466 --> 00:17:33.233
completely taut on the stretcher.

00:17:33.233 --> 00:17:34.400
[tapping]

00:17:35.266 --> 00:17:36.733
Once the canvas is taut,

00:17:36.733 --> 00:17:39.300
we use a water-based putty

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:42.633
to fill the cracks in the canvas' surface

00:17:42.633 --> 00:17:44.900
and fill in any areas along the edges

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:48.666
or wherever it might need to
have a surface evened out.

00:17:48.666 --> 00:17:51.300
I select the colors that I expect

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:53.966
I will need for retouching.

00:17:53.966 --> 00:17:56.466
We're using Gamblin conservation paints.

00:17:56.466 --> 00:17:58.500
These are paints that
are specifically designed

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.866
for conservation work, and they're soluble

00:18:00.866 --> 00:18:03.333
in solvents that are
different than solvents

00:18:03.333 --> 00:18:05.233
that will affect the oil paint.

00:18:05.233 --> 00:18:08.200
So, again, it's always very easy to remove

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:09.866
for whatever reason.

00:18:09.866 --> 00:18:12.966
I mix the colors to try to match

00:18:12.966 --> 00:18:16.333
the original color as best as possible.

00:18:16.333 --> 00:18:17.866
I'll put paint on the surface;

00:18:17.866 --> 00:18:19.800
if it looks correct, I'll continue.

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:22.800
If I need to alter it, I will.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:27.066
One of the issues with this
painting was that it has been

00:18:27.066 --> 00:18:30.666
previously overcleaned
by another restorer.

00:18:30.666 --> 00:18:33.133
To fix all this, I will

00:18:33.133 --> 00:18:36.300
remove the previous restorer's paint

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:40.866
and inpaint in just those areas of loss,

00:18:40.866 --> 00:18:44.100
where it's been, with our
own conservation paint,

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:47.566
stippling very carefully in
very, very, very small areas

00:18:47.566 --> 00:18:50.266
to make the painting look complete again.

00:18:50.266 --> 00:18:53.900
I'm very careful to only
put this conservation paint

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:55.066
in the areas of loss.

00:18:55.066 --> 00:18:57.900
I never want to cover
up any original paint

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:01.433
or do anything to obstruct
the original materials

00:19:01.433 --> 00:19:03.100
the artist put there.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:05.766
I inpaint the entirety of the canvas,

00:19:05.766 --> 00:19:10.166
and we do a final coat of varnish again.

00:19:11.466 --> 00:19:13.733
This final coat, it's the moment of truth.

00:19:13.733 --> 00:19:15.766
It brings out all the work,

00:19:15.766 --> 00:19:19.300
and once the final layer of
varnish is on and it dries,

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:21.633
the painting restoration is complete.

00:19:22.966 --> 00:19:25.433
Now there's the whole
frame restoration. [laughs]

00:19:25.433 --> 00:19:27.800
Caryn does an allover vacuuming

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:32.600
and then a sponge treatment
of the frame surface

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:34.933
to remove any loose dust.

00:19:34.933 --> 00:19:36.566
Now we clean the frame.

00:19:36.566 --> 00:19:39.266
We use the same surfactant soap

00:19:39.266 --> 00:19:41.966
that we use on the painting surface.

00:19:44.433 --> 00:19:47.233
Two of the four mitered
corners on the frame

00:19:47.233 --> 00:19:49.566
have very large gaps.

00:19:49.566 --> 00:19:52.766
So we first will use a epoxy putty

00:19:52.766 --> 00:19:55.166
to fill these areas,

00:19:55.166 --> 00:19:57.733
and then come in and fill over that

00:19:57.733 --> 00:19:59.866
with a water-based putty.

00:19:59.866 --> 00:20:02.500
We fill the large ones.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:03.700
It's always a judgment call,

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:07.966
but we leave the smaller ones
to retain an element of age.

00:20:07.966 --> 00:20:12.533
The ornament detail on two
corners of the frame are missing.

00:20:12.533 --> 00:20:16.433
We need to make a mold of
the ornament that does exist.

00:20:16.433 --> 00:20:21.433
Then we use a plaster to
actually cast a replacement.

00:20:21.433 --> 00:20:24.766
Caryn glues them down onto the frame,

00:20:24.766 --> 00:20:26.666
does a little bit more shaping,

00:20:26.666 --> 00:20:29.266
and then we're ready to retouch the frame.

00:20:29.266 --> 00:20:30.266
[machine whirring]

00:20:30.266 --> 00:20:31.666
Once we have the frame retouched,

00:20:31.666 --> 00:20:35.966
we apply shellac to protect our work

00:20:35.966 --> 00:20:38.733
and make sure it's all sealed.

00:20:38.733 --> 00:20:43.733
Now the painting and the frame
restorations are complete.

00:20:43.866 --> 00:20:46.400
We put the painting into the frame,

00:20:47.966 --> 00:20:51.166
and we hang the painting up on the wall,

00:20:51.166 --> 00:20:54.000
and admire our work. [laughs]

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:40.933
My team and I are going
to walk you through

00:21:40.933 --> 00:21:43.400
step by step how we restore

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:46.633
a 250-plus-year-old

00:21:46.633 --> 00:21:49.733
French plaster and giltwood frame.

00:21:49.733 --> 00:21:53.166
This frame was brought
to us by a private client

00:21:53.166 --> 00:21:56.233
along with a painting that
we will restore as well.

00:21:56.233 --> 00:21:58.200
The frame, when it came to us,

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:00.200
was extremely dirty,

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:04.066
and it needed a lot of structural repairs.

00:22:04.066 --> 00:22:06.633
There was a lot of
ornament that was missing.

00:22:13.033 --> 00:22:16.000
When we start cleaning the frame,

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:18.333
we start with the back.

00:22:18.333 --> 00:22:21.500
There's a lot of resin
on the back of the frame,

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:25.900
so we use a scalpel to
scrape this resin off.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.500
It's likely some sort of polyurethane

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:31.200
from a previous restoration.

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:32.866
That stuff is tricky to remove,

00:22:32.866 --> 00:22:35.666
so mechanically removing it with a scalpel

00:22:35.666 --> 00:22:37.500
is the best approach.

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:43.700
Once we have the back clean,

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:46.100
we flip the frame right side up

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:49.433
and we start the front cleaning.

00:22:52.933 --> 00:22:55.433
We commonly use cotton swabs

00:22:55.433 --> 00:22:59.200
to clean the surface of a frame,

00:23:00.833 --> 00:23:05.566
but we also are going to
be using micro-metal tools,

00:23:05.566 --> 00:23:09.166
literally the same tools
that a dentist will use

00:23:09.166 --> 00:23:14.133
to really get into all of
the small little recesses.

00:23:17.466 --> 00:23:20.800
We use a variety of solvents,

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:22.833
which lift the dirt up

00:23:22.833 --> 00:23:26.033
and allow us to then take swabs

00:23:26.033 --> 00:23:28.000
to lift the dirt away.

00:23:28.966 --> 00:23:33.133
This frame was originally water gilded,

00:23:33.133 --> 00:23:36.700
which means gold leaf was applied to it.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:38.066
Because of that, we have to be

00:23:38.066 --> 00:23:40.000
very careful with our cleaning,

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:42.800
because it would be very easy

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:45.533
to remove that original gilding.

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:52.233
We never want to make
something look brand new.

00:23:52.233 --> 00:23:55.533
So while we are going into the recesses

00:23:55.533 --> 00:23:57.466
and we are removing the dirt,

00:23:57.466 --> 00:24:00.266
we don't want to entirely remove

00:24:00.266 --> 00:24:02.466
all of that built-up patina.

00:24:02.466 --> 00:24:05.533
We want to retain an element of age.

00:24:13.733 --> 00:24:16.366
The main issue with the back of this frame

00:24:16.366 --> 00:24:20.000
is that it has been expanded

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:21.600
to take a larger piece of art

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.966
than it was originally built for.

00:24:25.066 --> 00:24:26.000
The first time we saw it,

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:29.533
we knew that we were going
to have to disassemble it

00:24:29.533 --> 00:24:32.033
and build it back up properly.

00:24:33.233 --> 00:24:37.166
In order to put the frame back properly,

00:24:37.166 --> 00:24:40.566
we are using a product called fish glue,

00:24:40.566 --> 00:24:44.566
which is a super-condensed fish product.

00:24:44.566 --> 00:24:46.733
It's a very traditional adhesive

00:24:46.733 --> 00:24:49.166
to use in conservation,
and it's very strong.

00:24:50.833 --> 00:24:53.400
And then we align the frame.

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:06.333
Once we feel that all the structural work

00:25:06.333 --> 00:25:07.900
has been completed,

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:10.633
we move on to filling in

00:25:10.633 --> 00:25:12.833
the missing areas of the frame.

00:25:14.033 --> 00:25:15.833
Three of the four corners

00:25:15.833 --> 00:25:19.466
are completely missing
their corner ornament.

00:25:19.466 --> 00:25:23.900
Luckily, we have one corner
that is completely intact.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:25.733
That works to our advantage in this case,

00:25:25.733 --> 00:25:27.066
because we will be able

00:25:27.066 --> 00:25:31.466
to take a silicone mold of this corner

00:25:31.466 --> 00:25:34.733
and then cast plaster replicas of it.

00:25:35.766 --> 00:25:38.166
To make a mold off the frame,

00:25:38.166 --> 00:25:41.166
we use a silicone compound.

00:25:41.166 --> 00:25:42.866
Once you have it mixed together,

00:25:42.866 --> 00:25:46.433
you can lay it over a part of the frame

00:25:46.433 --> 00:25:47.800
that you want to replicate.

00:25:51.633 --> 00:25:54.733
It cures over the course of about

00:25:54.733 --> 00:25:56.933
20 or 30 minutes.

00:25:56.933 --> 00:25:58.400
Once it's fully cured,

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:01.933
you can carefully peel it off the frame,

00:26:06.666 --> 00:26:10.733
and you will have the imprint
of the shape of the frame

00:26:10.733 --> 00:26:12.733
that you want to replicate in that mold.

00:26:14.933 --> 00:26:18.533
We are using plaster to cast the molds.

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:24.166
So we mix up a batch of plaster,

00:26:28.766 --> 00:26:31.900
and we pour it into the silicone mold.

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:40.666
Once the plaster has dried,

00:26:40.666 --> 00:26:43.833
we can pop that out of the mold.

00:26:50.166 --> 00:26:53.333
To prepare the frame to
receive the plaster casts,

00:26:53.333 --> 00:26:57.366
we need to remove some
of the plaster material

00:26:57.366 --> 00:26:59.333
that's already on it.

00:26:59.333 --> 00:27:01.866
We use a hammer and chisel

00:27:01.866 --> 00:27:06.566
to carve away a recess that will allow

00:27:06.566 --> 00:27:10.666
the cast piece to sit flush in the frame.

00:27:13.533 --> 00:27:17.833
We will shape and
finesse these cast pieces

00:27:17.833 --> 00:27:20.100
to make sure that they fit properly

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:23.133
in the areas where we're
going to be applying them.

00:27:23.133 --> 00:27:26.433
We use sandpaper in different grits.

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.966
We are using fish glue again

00:27:33.966 --> 00:27:38.200
to adhere the cast pieces onto the frame.

00:27:54.766 --> 00:27:59.533
When we feel like the frame
is physically complete,

00:27:59.533 --> 00:28:03.866
it is time to move on to the color work.

00:28:03.866 --> 00:28:05.700
The first layer of paint

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:09.900
that we are applying to
the frame is a primer.

00:28:10.933 --> 00:28:13.800
This is a Venetian red primer.

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:18.033
And that just creates a
very nice red base layer

00:28:18.033 --> 00:28:20.133
for us to work off of.

00:28:25.033 --> 00:28:28.666
We are gold-leafing the ornaments

00:28:28.666 --> 00:28:30.433
in the corners of this frame.

00:28:30.433 --> 00:28:32.700
This is a process called gilding.

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:39.400
The frame was originally
gilded with gold leaf.

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:40.900
And so whenever possible,

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:44.533
we like to use the original
materials in our restoration,

00:28:44.533 --> 00:28:47.366
just so the object has as much integrity

00:28:47.366 --> 00:28:49.733
to the original as possible.

00:28:49.733 --> 00:28:51.766
The first step to gilding

00:28:51.766 --> 00:28:53.500
after you've applied the base layer

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:57.800
is to apply a sizing medium.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:01.133
And this is brushed onto the plaster,

00:29:01.133 --> 00:29:05.633
and it is allowed to dry
for about 30 minutes.

00:29:07.833 --> 00:29:10.033
It gets tacky.

00:29:10.033 --> 00:29:12.133
And once it's tacky,

00:29:12.133 --> 00:29:16.733
it's in the right state
to accept the gold leaf.

00:29:16.733 --> 00:29:19.566
It is very, very delicate,

00:29:19.566 --> 00:29:23.500
so you can't touch it with your hands.

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:25.166
It will just crumble.

00:29:26.066 --> 00:29:27.733
So in order to handle it,

00:29:27.733 --> 00:29:29.866
Caryn, one of our team members,

00:29:29.866 --> 00:29:32.733
is using a horsehair brush

00:29:32.733 --> 00:29:37.100
and lay it onto the ornament
that we want to gild.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:40.400
We also will use squirrel-hair brushes

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:44.466
to brush it on and make
sure that it's adhering

00:29:44.466 --> 00:29:48.966
to all of the recesses and
all of the areas of the frame.

00:29:55.633 --> 00:29:57.633
Once the gold leaf is applied,

00:29:57.633 --> 00:30:00.933
we move on to the inpainting process.

00:30:00.933 --> 00:30:03.533
And we are going to
paint over the gold leaf

00:30:03.533 --> 00:30:05.366
to tone it down slightly,

00:30:05.366 --> 00:30:09.566
and also over all of the fills
that we've made on the frame.

00:30:09.566 --> 00:30:12.333
And we use a combination

00:30:12.333 --> 00:30:14.800
of conservation colors,

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:16.400
which are paints

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:19.033
that are specifically designed
for conservation work.

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:23.500
It is literally a trial-and-error process.

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:25.366
Often, we will make a color,

00:30:25.366 --> 00:30:28.433
we'll have to adjust it,
correct it a little bit.

00:30:31.333 --> 00:30:33.600
This is where the frame
starts to really look

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:35.766
like one whole object again.

00:30:37.833 --> 00:30:39.666
The next step is to

00:30:39.666 --> 00:30:43.166
apply a layer of shellac.

00:30:43.166 --> 00:30:45.733
And this just serves as a topcoat.

00:30:45.733 --> 00:30:47.566
It protects all of our work.

00:30:47.566 --> 00:30:49.666
It also makes everything

00:30:49.666 --> 00:30:52.466
just have a cohesive finish and sheen.

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:03.533
We're really happy with how
this restoration came out.

00:31:03.533 --> 00:31:05.933
We have done a lot

00:31:05.933 --> 00:31:09.300
to restore the structural integrity

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.600
and the visual integrity of this frame,

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:14.166
and we hope the client can enjoy the frame

00:31:14.166 --> 00:31:15.700
for years to come.

00:31:19.466 --> 00:31:23.066
[angelic orchestral music]

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:32.266
I am going to walk you
through how my team and I

00:31:32.266 --> 00:31:36.766
restore a 19th-century
Italian marble sculpture

00:31:36.766 --> 00:31:38.733
and its alabaster base.

00:31:38.733 --> 00:31:40.100
So it's been broken,

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:41.266
it's been previously repaired.

00:31:41.266 --> 00:31:43.733
It came to us broken again.

00:31:43.733 --> 00:31:46.500
This work of art is comprised

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:49.233
of four separate pieces.

00:31:49.233 --> 00:31:51.600
That includes the marble bust,

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:55.266
its socle base, and a pedestal,

00:31:55.266 --> 00:31:58.333
which is in two parts, a top and a column.

00:32:01.433 --> 00:32:04.333
We're going to clean the
bust, the socle base,

00:32:04.333 --> 00:32:07.000
and the pedestal
components all separately.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:10.100
We start out on all of the pieces

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:13.200
by using a surfactant detergent

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:15.933
diluted in distilled water

00:32:15.933 --> 00:32:19.733
and spray it onto the
marble and the alabaster.

00:32:19.733 --> 00:32:21.566
We want to use what we need

00:32:21.566 --> 00:32:23.600
to properly clean a piece,

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:26.066
but we never want to do anything

00:32:26.066 --> 00:32:27.400
that's too harsh.

00:32:27.400 --> 00:32:30.200
And this surfactant is very gentle,

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:33.533
and it lifts the dirt off the surface,

00:32:33.533 --> 00:32:35.000
kind of breaks the surface tension

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:36.833
between the dirt and the surface,

00:32:36.833 --> 00:32:41.433
and allows us to wipe or swab away

00:32:41.433 --> 00:32:45.233
the accumulated surface dirt and debris.

00:32:47.333 --> 00:32:50.100
The column is quite dirty,

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:51.733
and this is because a pedestal column

00:32:51.733 --> 00:32:53.000
sits on the floor

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:56.233
and the dirt from the
floor, from people's shoes,

00:32:56.233 --> 00:33:00.166
it all gets brought up onto that surface.

00:33:00.166 --> 00:33:03.500
We also use surfactant gels,

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:06.033
particularly in areas that might be

00:33:06.033 --> 00:33:08.000
a little bit more stubborn to clean.

00:33:09.666 --> 00:33:11.933
When restoring something so old,

00:33:11.933 --> 00:33:15.900
you have to be mindful
of the age of the piece.

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:19.700
So we never want to remove all of the dirt

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:21.333
out of all of the recesses

00:33:21.333 --> 00:33:24.400
and completely remove the patina

00:33:24.400 --> 00:33:27.900
that's built up on an item of age

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.133
because that would end up

00:33:29.133 --> 00:33:31.100
looking incorrect and inappropriate.

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:39.966
So the top of the pedestal
and the socle base

00:33:39.966 --> 00:33:41.933
that the bust sits directly on

00:33:41.933 --> 00:33:45.633
have both been previously repaired twice

00:33:45.633 --> 00:33:47.800
with the same types of glue.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:50.266
We need to entirely remove

00:33:50.266 --> 00:33:52.400
all of this old adhesive

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:55.033
that's not functional, it's yellowing,

00:33:55.033 --> 00:33:56.900
it's not good for the marble

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:58.500
to have on its surface.

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:00.100
So we want to make sure
we get all of it off.

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:05.100
So we are using a combination of scalpels.

00:34:05.333 --> 00:34:06.700
This is also a time

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:07.933
where you have to be very careful

00:34:07.933 --> 00:34:10.433
because marble is very susceptible

00:34:10.433 --> 00:34:12.566
and alabaster is very, very susceptible

00:34:12.566 --> 00:34:13.400
to scratching.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:15.800
So when you're using
those very sharp tools

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:18.166
you want to make sure

00:34:18.166 --> 00:34:20.566
that you are only scraping off the glue

00:34:20.566 --> 00:34:23.066
and you're not accidentally nicking

00:34:23.066 --> 00:34:25.133
the surrounding surface area.

00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:35.033
We use a number of visual aids to help us

00:34:35.033 --> 00:34:37.066
in the conservation process.

00:34:37.066 --> 00:34:39.866
One tool that's very helpful

00:34:39.866 --> 00:34:42.266
is a small spotlight.

00:34:42.266 --> 00:34:44.833
This is a very direct, targeted light

00:34:44.833 --> 00:34:47.833
that allows us to very
clearly see the area

00:34:47.833 --> 00:34:49.333
that we are working on.

00:34:50.633 --> 00:34:54.600
We also use a black light,
which is very useful

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:57.166
because the UV light

00:34:57.166 --> 00:35:00.466
allows us to see imperfections

00:35:00.466 --> 00:35:02.633
and residues on the surface

00:35:02.633 --> 00:35:07.266
that are not visible to
the regular human eye.

00:35:12.900 --> 00:35:16.533
To attach the broken items back together,

00:35:16.533 --> 00:35:20.933
we are going to use a
special conservation epoxy.

00:35:20.933 --> 00:35:24.900
It is called Epo-Tek.
This is a two-part epoxy

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:25.900
that you combine together.

00:35:25.900 --> 00:35:29.266
When you first mix it,
it can be quite liquidy,

00:35:29.266 --> 00:35:33.466
so we allow it to sit
for a little bit of time

00:35:33.466 --> 00:35:35.366
just to develop little bit more

00:35:35.366 --> 00:35:37.166
of a viscous quality.

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:42.933
We are going to permanently
attach the marble

00:35:42.933 --> 00:35:44.366
to the socle base.

00:35:44.366 --> 00:35:48.833
We're going to reuse
the original metal rod.

00:35:48.833 --> 00:35:51.133
Once we have it at the right depth,

00:35:51.133 --> 00:35:54.633
we put the pin in the hole of the bust

00:35:54.633 --> 00:35:59.600
and we use our epoxy,
pour that into the recess.

00:36:11.233 --> 00:36:14.033
And then we put the base on the marble,

00:36:14.033 --> 00:36:15.866
check it for alignment,

00:36:15.866 --> 00:36:17.833
front to back and left to right,

00:36:17.833 --> 00:36:22.333
and allow that to cure for a little while.

00:36:24.066 --> 00:36:26.266
To ensure that the repair

00:36:26.266 --> 00:36:28.833
of the top of the pedestal
is extremely strong,

00:36:28.833 --> 00:36:32.233
we are going to pin the pieces

00:36:32.233 --> 00:36:34.633
in addition to epoxying them.

00:36:34.633 --> 00:36:38.133
We are going to take
a stainless-steel rod.

00:36:38.133 --> 00:36:40.266
Stainless steel is desirable

00:36:40.266 --> 00:36:42.000
because it's not going to rust

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:44.233
or change over time.

00:36:44.233 --> 00:36:48.333
We mark on the outside of the piece

00:36:48.333 --> 00:36:51.333
where we're going to drill holes

00:36:51.333 --> 00:36:53.400
to take the pins.

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:56.833
And we are using our ancient drill press.

00:37:02.766 --> 00:37:06.233
Once we have the holes, we have the pins,

00:37:06.233 --> 00:37:10.333
we then can use our epoxy

00:37:10.333 --> 00:37:14.166
to attach the two pieces back together.

00:37:26.166 --> 00:37:28.400
We are going to fill the cracks

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:31.166
in the sculpture with plaster.

00:37:35.833 --> 00:37:38.066
We are applying the plaster

00:37:38.066 --> 00:37:39.933
with small metal tools.

00:37:39.933 --> 00:37:43.400
We tend to err on the side of overfilling

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:45.100
because it's very easy

00:37:45.100 --> 00:37:47.766
to remove the plaster material.

00:37:47.766 --> 00:37:49.766
The plaster cures quickly, so you want

00:37:49.766 --> 00:37:51.833
to make sure that you're getting enough on

00:37:51.833 --> 00:37:53.833
while it's still workable.

00:37:56.266 --> 00:37:57.933
Once we've filled the cracks

00:37:57.933 --> 00:38:02.466
and we've allowed the
plaster to fully dry,

00:38:02.466 --> 00:38:05.200
we are going to sand,

00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:08.400
particularly the alabaster base,

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:10.300
which had a large crack

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:11.400
throughout the entire thing.

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:13.433
We are going to sand that down

00:38:13.433 --> 00:38:16.233
with different grits of sandpaper.

00:38:23.833 --> 00:38:26.700
In painting, a marble sculpture

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:29.200
is really the same technique that we use

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:32.333
for any other sculpture or a painting.

00:38:32.333 --> 00:38:36.400
We are using our standard
Gamblin Conservation Colors.

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:39.133
We mix them up to get the right color.

00:38:39.133 --> 00:38:40.466
In this instance,

00:38:40.466 --> 00:38:43.166
because the marble and the alabaster

00:38:43.166 --> 00:38:45.166
are sort of translucent,

00:38:45.166 --> 00:38:46.833
it makes our job a little trickier

00:38:46.833 --> 00:38:49.366
because we're trying to
replicate translucency

00:38:49.366 --> 00:38:51.400
on an opaque fill.

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:53.933
In this instance, you
really can't mix one color

00:38:53.933 --> 00:38:55.466
and apply it to the whole fill.

00:38:55.466 --> 00:38:59.400
You have to do thin
washes of different colors

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:03.100
to mimic the texture and the veining

00:39:03.100 --> 00:39:05.633
of the stone material.

00:39:05.633 --> 00:39:09.300
We are going to polish the alabaster base,

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:13.066
and we use a very large
polishing wheel for this.

00:39:13.066 --> 00:39:17.433
And we use polishing compound
applied to the wheel,

00:39:17.433 --> 00:39:20.066
and that polishing compound comes

00:39:20.066 --> 00:39:21.466
in a variety of grits.

00:39:21.466 --> 00:39:24.266
We use very fine ones in this instance

00:39:24.266 --> 00:39:26.533
so it doesn't damage the alabaster.

00:39:29.233 --> 00:39:32.233
The final step of the conservation

00:39:32.233 --> 00:39:37.233
is to wax each piece of the sculpture.

00:39:37.733 --> 00:39:39.433
So we are using

00:39:39.433 --> 00:39:42.866
a very, very fine microcrystalline wax.

00:39:42.866 --> 00:39:45.300
And we apply this

00:39:45.300 --> 00:39:49.066
over the whole surface of
each one of the pieces,

00:39:49.066 --> 00:39:51.000
allow it to dry for just a little bit,

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:53.300
and then buff it off very gently

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:55.500
with soft cloths.

00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:57.133
This wax is important.

00:39:57.133 --> 00:40:02.133
It not only makes the
stone look good visually,

00:40:02.333 --> 00:40:05.433
it also serves as a protective layer

00:40:05.433 --> 00:40:09.366
between the stone and the elements.

00:40:09.366 --> 00:40:11.100
Once all of the components

00:40:11.100 --> 00:40:13.300
are complete in their own right,

00:40:13.300 --> 00:40:15.700
we do a final assembly, and this is how

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:20.566
the piece will be ultimately displayed

00:40:20.566 --> 00:40:22.066
at the client's home.

00:40:29.033 --> 00:40:30.133
We are very satisfied

00:40:30.133 --> 00:40:32.133
with how the whole piece came out.

00:40:32.133 --> 00:40:34.433
We know the client loves this piece

00:40:34.433 --> 00:40:37.700
and has had it broken for a long time.

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:39.633
We think she's going be very happy.

00:40:39.633 --> 00:40:42.800
[bright, jaunty music]

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:45.033
[soft music]

00:40:47.366 --> 00:40:50.900
[light scraping]

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:53.566
[water pouring]

00:40:57.633 --> 00:40:59.400
My name is Sophia Bogle.

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:01.633
I've been restoring
books for over 25 years,

00:41:01.633 --> 00:41:04.166
and I started my company, Save Your Books,

00:41:04.166 --> 00:41:07.066
so that anyone can learn
archival-book repair.

00:41:07.066 --> 00:41:09.600
The book I'm restoring today is

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.400
the Hans Christian
Andersen "Fairy Tales" book

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:16.600
from the late 1800s, early 1900s.

00:41:16.600 --> 00:41:19.100
[soft music]

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:21.633
The first thing that we do

00:41:21.633 --> 00:41:24.266
in pretty much any full restoration

00:41:24.266 --> 00:41:26.466
is we have to separate the cover

00:41:26.466 --> 00:41:28.200
from the text block.

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:30.800
The tool that I prefer to use

00:41:30.800 --> 00:41:32.700
is the book-repair knife.

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:34.933
It has a rounded tip

00:41:34.933 --> 00:41:39.100
and is less likely to do
damage that you did not intend

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:41.133
as an X-Acto knife would.

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:43.800
I'm removing the old tape.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:44.866
It's very acidic,

00:41:44.866 --> 00:41:46.700
and if I leave it on the book at all,

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:48.833
it's going to affect the book later on.

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:54.266
When you're removing some
of the original material

00:41:54.266 --> 00:41:55.166
and you know you're going to be

00:41:55.166 --> 00:41:56.266
replacing it with something,

00:41:56.266 --> 00:41:58.600
it's good to mark where it was

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:02.000
in relationship to the other materials.

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:02.900
And so in this case,

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:05.966
I've used a pencil and a piece of paper

00:42:05.966 --> 00:42:09.166
to mark where the original went.

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:14.566
I'm using a 100-grit sanding block

00:42:14.566 --> 00:42:17.766
to clean the edges of these boards off.

00:42:17.766 --> 00:42:19.666
It makes a nice, clean edge.

00:42:19.666 --> 00:42:22.100
[soft music]

00:42:27.900 --> 00:42:31.433
[light scraping]

00:42:32.933 --> 00:42:35.433
A lot of general cleaning can be done

00:42:35.433 --> 00:42:37.400
using a book-repair knife,

00:42:37.400 --> 00:42:39.100
and using the book-repair knife

00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:41.433
to just remove some clumpy bits

00:42:41.433 --> 00:42:44.133
that are stuck to the end paper.

00:42:44.133 --> 00:42:45.400
[water pouring]

00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:46.700
When you're repairing a book,

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:48.966
usually all the pages are still connected,

00:42:48.966 --> 00:42:51.900
and there are some pages that will benefit

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:54.700
from this washing technique that I have.

00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:57.966
It'll help us to get the
tape off of the page,

00:42:57.966 --> 00:42:59.300
in a way that you couldn't do

00:42:59.300 --> 00:43:01.366
if it was attached to the book still.

00:43:01.366 --> 00:43:03.100
To wash a page,

00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:06.633
to get rid of the tape,

00:43:06.633 --> 00:43:09.966
you just have to put
it in fairly hot water.

00:43:10.966 --> 00:43:15.300
You aren't going to need any
additives to clean these pages.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:17.000
It's just hot water.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:18.866
It's surprisingly effective.

00:43:19.733 --> 00:43:22.900
It can sit in the water for hours.

00:43:24.233 --> 00:43:25.733
You're also just going to have to be

00:43:25.733 --> 00:43:28.066
very delicate with the paper,

00:43:28.066 --> 00:43:29.866
because it's in a more delicate state

00:43:29.866 --> 00:43:31.400
when it's in the water.

00:43:32.700 --> 00:43:36.433
I'm using a microspatula
to help separate the pages,

00:43:36.433 --> 00:43:38.666
and I'm also using a ruler

00:43:38.666 --> 00:43:41.400
to help hold down the other page.

00:43:43.533 --> 00:43:45.500
You can use a soft brush

00:43:45.500 --> 00:43:48.700
to help you to brush
away the old adhesive.

00:43:49.966 --> 00:43:52.466
[soft music]

00:44:09.133 --> 00:44:12.500
When you're touching up
anything black on a cover,

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:15.633
you're going to want
to use an archival pen.

00:44:15.633 --> 00:44:18.266
It's a 0.08-micron pen,

00:44:18.266 --> 00:44:20.933
and it is archival ink.

00:44:20.933 --> 00:44:23.333
The smaller the tip, the better, in a way.

00:44:23.333 --> 00:44:24.900
Do not use a Sharpie.

00:44:24.900 --> 00:44:28.033
Those will damage and discolor.

00:44:28.033 --> 00:44:30.533
[soft music]

00:44:31.866 --> 00:44:34.166
While I was cleaning,

00:44:34.166 --> 00:44:37.766
I also noticed that some
of the gilt stamping

00:44:37.766 --> 00:44:39.966
has been removed.

00:44:41.333 --> 00:44:43.000
When I'm using the word "gilt,"

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:44.733
gilt refers to gold.

00:44:44.733 --> 00:44:47.133
So it's gold stamping on the cover.

00:44:47.133 --> 00:44:49.200
Some people would call it embossing.

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:55.200
Originally, this was stamped
with hot metal plates,

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:58.300
with foil coming down onto the book.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:02.033
The best way to fill in gilt stamping

00:45:02.033 --> 00:45:03.900
is to use shell gold.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:08.100
I'm using a liner brush,

00:45:08.100 --> 00:45:10.100
and I have all the control in the world.

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:12.600
You're going to make mistakes.

00:45:12.600 --> 00:45:15.700
You can actually remove the gilt

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:17.966
by using a cuticle stick,

00:45:17.966 --> 00:45:22.133
and then you can clean up your gilt.

00:45:23.566 --> 00:45:26.066
[soft music]

00:45:30.033 --> 00:45:33.633
If you want to match
the color of your book

00:45:33.633 --> 00:45:35.300
and do touch-up on the covers,

00:45:35.300 --> 00:45:37.933
it's not as hard as you might think.

00:45:37.933 --> 00:45:40.900
You look at this, this
is a greenish brown.

00:45:40.900 --> 00:45:45.700
The brownish colors that tend
to be on these older books,

00:45:45.700 --> 00:45:47.700
they're muddy looking.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:51.200
And the way to get to
that muddy-looking color

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:53.866
is to go across the color wheel

00:45:53.866 --> 00:45:56.100
from the color you think it is

00:45:56.100 --> 00:46:00.300
and add that other color to
make it more muddy looking.

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:02.800
[soft music]

00:46:14.933 --> 00:46:17.500
When you find tears on your pages

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:19.100
and you want to repair them,

00:46:19.100 --> 00:46:23.666
I highly recommend using
Japanese tissue that's thin

00:46:23.666 --> 00:46:25.566
and an archival paste,

00:46:25.566 --> 00:46:28.033
such as what I have here,
which is nori paste.

00:46:28.033 --> 00:46:29.866
It's a rice-starch paste.

00:46:29.866 --> 00:46:32.500
A really thin tissue is going to be better

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:34.133
than a thicker tissue.

00:46:34.133 --> 00:46:36.033
You don't need very much paste.

00:46:37.033 --> 00:46:40.266
[soft music]

00:46:41.800 --> 00:46:44.333
You get everything ready.

00:46:44.333 --> 00:46:47.233
You've got your book in the cover,

00:46:47.233 --> 00:46:48.533
right side up.

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:52.633
You're going to be gluing
all of those materials.

00:46:52.633 --> 00:46:56.533
You're going to glue that
mull and that little hinge,

00:46:56.533 --> 00:46:59.133
and you're gluing that
decorative end sheet back,

00:46:59.133 --> 00:47:02.100
and then you're going to close the cover.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:05.900
[soft music]

00:47:07.033 --> 00:47:10.200
And then you slip a sheet of wax paper in.

00:47:12.366 --> 00:47:14.600
And then you put the pressing board in.

00:47:19.533 --> 00:47:20.766
When I discovered that there were

00:47:20.766 --> 00:47:22.700
pages missing from this book,

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:26.866
I found a version of
the story that connected

00:47:26.866 --> 00:47:31.400
to where the book started
with the pages missing.

00:47:32.333 --> 00:47:35.600
I used paper that's
called antique endleaf.

00:47:36.666 --> 00:47:38.133
[soft music]

00:47:38.133 --> 00:47:42.000
I am using a colored
pencil to color the edges

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:45.766
of the new pages that I added to the book.

00:47:45.766 --> 00:47:47.500
I don't want them to pop out.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:49.100
I don't want it to look obvious

00:47:49.100 --> 00:47:51.000
that I added these new pages.

00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:53.500
[soft music]

00:47:56.133 --> 00:47:57.533
And that's it.

00:47:57.533 --> 00:47:59.100
That's our book.

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:01.533
[soft music]

00:48:22.833 --> 00:48:25.266
My name is Andrea Sozzi Sabatini,

00:48:25.266 --> 00:48:27.633
and I'm the CEO and founder of Etrurio.

00:48:27.633 --> 00:48:31.133
It's a farm where it aims to produce

00:48:31.133 --> 00:48:33.233
top-quality olive oil in Tuscany.

00:48:33.233 --> 00:48:35.300
Today, I'm going to walk you through

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:38.633
how Marco restores this beautiful

00:48:38.633 --> 00:48:41.600
583-year-old fresco.

00:48:45.766 --> 00:48:47.600
The first work that we have to do

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:49.900
is to consolidate the walls.

00:48:49.900 --> 00:48:53.966
The first thing is
really to take the stones

00:48:53.966 --> 00:48:56.366
that are unstable, take them off,

00:48:56.366 --> 00:48:58.866
consolidate whatever is underneath,

00:48:58.866 --> 00:49:03.433
and then put the stones
back to their places.

00:49:05.800 --> 00:49:09.066
In this large fresco, there
are cracks here and there,

00:49:09.066 --> 00:49:13.300
so the next step is to
consolidate these cracks.

00:49:13.300 --> 00:49:15.933
The cracks get consolidated

00:49:15.933 --> 00:49:18.300
with resin, special resin.

00:49:20.166 --> 00:49:23.033
The next step is now
to remove the plasters

00:49:23.033 --> 00:49:25.600
that had been painted over the fresco

00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:27.366
in the last few centuries.

00:49:27.366 --> 00:49:29.466
And to do that, Marco, the restorer,

00:49:29.466 --> 00:49:32.233
needs to get archeological scalpel

00:49:32.233 --> 00:49:35.066
and remove every single bit and pieces

00:49:35.066 --> 00:49:39.133
out of this huge surface.

00:49:39.133 --> 00:49:41.666
And it's very delicate work,

00:49:41.666 --> 00:49:43.900
because if you are not precise enough

00:49:43.900 --> 00:49:46.966
of course you can damage
the fresco itself.

00:49:53.333 --> 00:49:55.733
Now that the plaster has been removed,

00:49:55.733 --> 00:49:59.166
we can apply Japanese paper

00:49:59.166 --> 00:50:03.600
soaked with water and ammonium carbonate.

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:06.833
This is a very delicate process.

00:50:06.833 --> 00:50:08.833
And the reason why we
are doing it is because,

00:50:08.833 --> 00:50:10.833
thanks to the Japanese paper,

00:50:10.833 --> 00:50:14.866
all the dirt and all
the external component

00:50:14.866 --> 00:50:17.366
get away from the fresco.

00:50:17.366 --> 00:50:20.700
And it's a process that
can last up to six months.

00:50:20.700 --> 00:50:22.566
After all this work, as you can see,

00:50:22.566 --> 00:50:24.266
the fresco is revealing itself.

00:50:24.266 --> 00:50:27.066
Some of the colors are now vivid.

00:50:27.066 --> 00:50:29.500
What was underneath the plaster

00:50:29.500 --> 00:50:33.600
fortunately is still there for most of it.

00:50:33.600 --> 00:50:36.433
We are actually applying
again Japanese paper

00:50:36.433 --> 00:50:40.200
soaked with a different
product, carbonate hydroxide.

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:44.266
And this is a very important
part of the fresco restoration,

00:50:44.266 --> 00:50:47.666
because it stabilize the fresco itself.

00:50:55.233 --> 00:50:57.833
Now that all these
processes are completed,

00:50:57.833 --> 00:51:02.200
Marco can start the second
phase of the restoration.

00:51:02.200 --> 00:51:03.033
As you can see,

00:51:03.033 --> 00:51:05.100
there's a lot of gaps in the fresco,

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:07.466
because some of the paint has faded,

00:51:07.466 --> 00:51:08.833
some others were lost.

00:51:08.833 --> 00:51:13.000
He's going to use a
very tiny, small brush,

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:16.166
and he is going to use a natural pigment,

00:51:16.166 --> 00:51:19.533
like they were used back in the 1400s.

00:51:19.533 --> 00:51:20.700
There has been a surveillance

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:24.200
of the Italian authority over this fresco.

00:51:24.200 --> 00:51:25.500
We are not allowed in Italy

00:51:25.500 --> 00:51:28.866
to paint back anything
that is not present.

00:51:35.666 --> 00:51:38.366
The whole restoration took a long time.

00:51:38.366 --> 00:51:39.966
It took 18 months.

00:51:39.966 --> 00:51:41.866
We are very proud that

00:51:41.866 --> 00:51:45.533
we didn't lose this
piece of art in Tuscany.

